    "case_id": "30",
    "case_summary": "A 15-year-old girl comes to the physician because of episodic pelvic pain radiating to her back and thighs for 4 months. The pain occurs a few hours before her menstrual period and lasts for 2 days. She has been taking ibuprofen, which has provided some relief. Menses have occurred at regular 28-day intervals since menarche at the age of 12 years and last for 5 to 6 days. She is sexually active with two male partners and uses condoms inconsistently. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows no abnormalities. What is the most likely cause of this patient's symptoms?\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. dysmenorrhea\nB. endometriosis\nC. pelvic inflammatory disease (PID)\nD. ovarian cyst\nE. sexually transmitted infection (STI)\nF. None\nG. None\n",
